00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:35.680
so factorial we'll write n factorial and it's the&nbsp;
product of n- so n times (n - 1) times... and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:41.840
on times 2 times 1. So it's the product of n&nbsp;
and all the integers less than n. So that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.000
the factorial, but there are other things we can&nbsp;
do with factorials as well. So there's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.960
called a double factorial and what we do here is&nbsp;
rather than multiplying everything we multiply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.960 --> 00:01:01.400
every other term. So it'll be n and then this time&nbsp;
instead it's n take 2 time n take 4 times dot dot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:06.960
dot. And now what we end up with will depend&nbsp;
on whether n's odd or even; cos if n is even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:14.360
then we'll have times 4 times 2 and if n is odd&nbsp;
it will be times 3 times 1 at the end. So that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:22.160
double factorial; but I said super factorial. So&nbsp;
what's that? Well the definition I'm going to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.800
different - mathematicians use super factorial to&nbsp;
mean different things - but I'm going to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:34.520
n super factorial, which is written like this is,&nbsp;
rather than being n times n take 1 times and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:44.640
on; n factorial times n take 1 factorial times dot&nbsp;
dot dot times 2 factorial times 1 factorial. Okay.&nbsp;

